<powershell>
# Set execution policy
Set-ExecutionPolicy Unrestricted -Force

# Install IIS Web Server if your app is an IIS-based web app
Install-WindowsFeature -name Web-Server -IncludeManagementTools

# Download your Go binary from S3
$ErrorActionPreference = "Stop"
Import-Module AWSPowerShell
Read-S3Object -BucketName 'your-bucket-name' -Key 'your-app.exe' -File 'C:\path\to\your-app.exe'

# Alternatively, use Invoke-WebRequest if the file is publicly accessible
# Invoke-WebRequest -Uri http://example.com/your-app.exe -OutFile C:\path\to\your-app.exe

# Add your application to the startup routine
$WShell = New-Object -comObject WScript.Shell
$Shortcut = $WShell.CreateShortcut("$env:APPDATA\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\Startup\YourApp.lnk")
$Shortcut.TargetPath = "C:\path\to\your-app.exe"
$Shortcut.Save()

# Start your application
Start-Process "C:\path\to\your-app.exe"
</powershell>
Step 2: Launch an EC2 Instance with User-Data
When you launch your EC2 instance through the AWS Management Console, CLI, or an API:

AWS Management Console:
Navigate to EC2 service.
Click “Launch Instance”.
Select your Windows AMI and instance type.
Configure instance details. In the “Advanced Details” section at the bottom, paste your PowerShell script into the “User data” field as text.
Add storage, configure security group (allow HTTP/HTTPS traffic if it's a web server), and select the key pair.
Review and launch the instance.
AWS CLI:
You can also specify user data via the AWS CLI by encoding your script in base64 and including it in the --user-data parameter when running aws ec2 run-instances. Example:
bash
Copy code
aws ec2 run-instances --image-id ami-0abcdef1234567890 --count 1 --instance-type t2.micro --key-name your-key-pair --security-group-ids sg-0123456789abcdef0 --subnet-id subnet-1234abcd --user-data file://path-to-your-script.txt
Step 3: Test and Verify
Once your instance is up and running, you should verify:

The application has been downloaded and installed correctly.
The web server (if applicable) is running and configured correctly.
Your application starts as expected.
You can connect to your instance using RDP to check these aspects or automate checks using additional scripts or monitoring tools.

